{
  "statement": "A row is a task, an event, a location, a link or something they handed over; a sentence is a note, a date does not make it an event, a standing rule is a note rather than a task, and something that comes round again is one row with a rule.",
  "why": "An event goes in the one place people expect things they have committed to with other people, so calling a plan an appointment puts it there wrongly. A rule that becomes a task is noise in a list; a task that becomes a rule is a lost todo. Four rows saying Yoga are four rows saying the same thing.",
  "sources": [
    "006 AC-1, AC-2, AC-5",
    "014: a note is a sentence about the thread",
    "plan_v2.md: What something is, Thread notes",
    "tests/test_truthfulness.py: a rule becomes a note and a task does not"
  ],
  "cases": [
